3. Working of Circuit Diagram  (image in folder)
The circuit operates as a non-programmable, hardware-driven automatic switch. It uses the infrared output logic of an HC-SR501 motion sensor to control the switching state of a BC547 NPN bipolar junction transistor (BJT).
•	Standby State (No Motion): The PIR sensor constantly scans its detection field. When no human motion is detected, the output pin (OUT) remains at 0V (LOW). Without base voltage, zero current flows into the base of the BC547 transistor. The transistor stays in the Cut-off state (acting as an open switch), and the LED load remains OFF.
•	Trigger Phase (Motion Detected): When a warm body enters the detection zone (3 to 7 meters), the PIR sensor detects the change in infrared radiation and immediately drives its OUT pin HIGH to 3.3V.
•	Transistor Saturation: The 3.3V logic signal passes through the 100 Ω base resistor (R1) into the base terminal of the BC547 transistor. This base current (I_B) drives the transistor out of cut-off and into Saturation mode (acting as a closed switch with V_CE ≈ 0.2V).
•	Load Conduction: With the transistor saturated, a path to ground is completed. Current flows from the +9V supply through the 330 Ω current-limiting resistor (R2), through the LED, and down to GND via the collector-emitter junction, turning the LED ON.
•	Auto-Reset Delay: Once motion ceases, the internal timer of the HC-SR501 holds the OUT signal HIGH for a preset delay (adjustable via potentiometer). After the delay elapses, OUT returns to 0V, turning off base current and switching the LED OFF.

4. Hardware Components
Component	Specification / Rating	Role in Circuit
HC-SR501 PIR Sensor	Operating: 4.5V–12V DC
Output: 3.3V High / 0V Low
Range: 3m–7m	Senses infrared thermal movement and sends logic HIGH output signal.
BC547 NPN Transistor	V_CEO = 45V, I_C = 100mA
Package: TO-92	Acts as an electronic solid-state switch triggered by PIR signal.
Resistor R1 (Base)	100 Ω (1/4 Watt)	Limits base current (I_B) to protect transistor and PIR output pin.
Resistor R2 (LED)	330 Ω (1/4 Watt)	Limits forward current through LED to prevent burning out.
LED / Power Supply	5mm Standard LED
9V DC Battery / Adapter	Serves as light output indicator and main circuit DC power source.

5. System Algorithm & Logic Flow
Sequential Operational Logic Flow:
•	Step 1 (Initialize): Power ON system (+9V DC). Allow 30–60 seconds warm-up time for PIR sensor calibration.
•	Step 2 (Sense): Monitor infrared radiation changes continuously in the coverage area.
•	Step 3 (Decision): If IR change > threshold, set PIR OUT = 3.3V (HIGH). Else, keep PIR OUT = 0V (LOW).
•	Step 4 (Switch): If PIR OUT = HIGH -> Base Current flows -> Transistor Saturates -> LED turns ON.
